Module sennet "The Grand Tour" {
  // ── When the book closes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // At nine on the morning after the Door of Doors is opened or Cloudgate comes down, or on the eighth morning,
  // whichever is first. What became of each of the city's people is told an hour before, as the morning's news
  // (aftermath.kyr); the settlement is the city's answer to what was taken, and what was given back.
  Measure decisiveDay(place).
  Rule decided when (doorOpen(sennet_bay) or cloudgateFell(sennet_bay)) and decisiveDay(sennet_bay) == 0 { Set decisiveDay(sennet_bay) to day(clock). }
  Query closingDay() means (decisiveDay(sennet_bay) >= 1 and day(clock) > decisiveDay(sennet_bay)) or day(clock) >= 8.
  Query closing() means closingDay() and hour(clock) >= 9.

  Ending end_accomplice "First Through" when closing() and accomplice(party) "You went through the Door of Doors first, as she promised, with the Rector of the House of Doors laughing at your elbow, and the wind of everywhere in your face; and you came back, as the Door lets you. She did not. Behind you, while you went, a hill came down out of the sky into the bay. In the morning Sennet does not know who took the petal out of the keel, nor yet how many of the sea-folk were under the hill when it came down, and the Door of Doors knows you, and will open for you on anywhere you have stood, for as long as you live. Every place you think of is there on the other side of it. You find that there are a great many places you would rather not see again.".
  Ending end_second_whelming "The Second Whelming" when closing() and cloudgateFell(sennet_bay) and not whelmEmptied(sennet_bay) "In the morning Halyard Hill is standing in the bay where it stood two hundred years ago, wet to its first floors, its gardens hanging off it in ropes of weed, a hundred yards off the end of the Fish Stairs; and under it is the Whelm. Chandlers' Row, and Lir's church with its bell, and the Guildhall with the arms of the Glovers' Company, and the merfolk who lived in them, who had never in their lives been told that anything was coming. Some got through the Water Door. Not many. All morning things come up on the tide at the Fish Stairs: a teapot, a drowned armchair, a basket of cockles. The harbour stands on the Quay and watches the water and says nothing, not even the thing it says when the sea is up. Cloudgate's people come across on the fishermen's boats, in their pale clothes, wet, and stand on the Quay among them, and for once nobody in Sennet can tell which is which.".
  Ending end_coming_down "The Coming Down" when closing() and cloudgateFell(sennet_bay) "In the morning Halyard Hill is an island in the bay, a hundred yards off the end of the Fish Stairs, wet to the first floor, with its orange trees standing in the sea; and the Whelm under it is empty. No Admittance took her people home through the Water Door in the hour before it came down, the whole Whelm in a long line through the green, children and old men and the lamps they could carry, the first time in three generations, and the Door closed behind them. Ha'penny came up to the third step before she went, to say goodbye to the Quay, and left her basket on it. Now the Fish Stairs go down into rock. A ferry runs to Cloudgate from the end of the Quay for a penny; the Halyards walk on the Quay like anybody, and buy eel pies, and are cheated, like anybody. It is not always afternoon there any more. It is whatever it is in Sennet.".
  Ending end_rectors_door "The Rector's Door" when closing() and rectorThrough(quance) and not firstThrough(party) "The Rector of the House of Doors went first through the Door of Doors, in her gown of four colours, with the wind of everywhere lifting the hair off her forehead, and did not come back on this side. The House has a new Rector by the morning, a nervous man from the Earth faculty who will not go up the tower stair. Nobody knows where she went. The Courant prints the last part of Thresholds on Saturday, as dictated, and it ends at the door. Some say she is somewhere very far off, being first at things. Some say the Door of Doors does not open on anywhere for a person who has done what she did; only on everywhere, which is not the same, and which you cannot come back from. The Grand Tour is declared closed. Its prize has been taken.".
  Ending end_made_petal "A Present from Sennet" when closing() and madeSet(sennet_bay) "In the morning Cloudgate is in the sky, where it has always been, at the top of its chain, in the sun above the cloud; and it is held up there by a petal of Lamp Street glass with a fern of frost in it, made in a night by a woman with no eyebrows, and stamped on the back, very small, A PRESENT FROM SENNET. Prue Quickly sends the Halyards her account, which is very long, and includes the heat, the night, the frost on the street and the breakage, and the Halyards pay it, because there is nobody else in the world who can make another. The winch-gang has had its third. Cloudgate is now, in a manner of speaking, in trade. Down on the Quay they think this the best joke in two hundred years, and they are right.".
  Ending end_gift_renewed "The Keepsake" when closing() and giftRenewed(sennet_bay) and imogenSorry(imogen) "It snowed on Sennet in June: big soft flakes out of a clear sky, on the Quay and the glass-houses and the bay, on Cloudgate's orange trees, on the brass plate on the third step of the Fish Stairs. By morning it has gone, and Cloudgate is still in the sky, held up by a keepsake that is not its own, by leave. The Halyards know it now, and so does the harbour, because the sylphs told everyone. Lady Halyard came down the chain in the night and stood on the Quay among the harbour's candles with one of her own, which she did not know how to light, and Old Bethan lit it for her without a word. Nobody in Sennet is sure yet what it means that she came. Nobody in Cloudgate is sure either.".
  Ending end_letter "The Letter" when closing() and giftRenewed(sennet_bay) "It snowed on Sennet in June: big soft flakes out of a clear sky, on the Quay and the glass-houses and the bay, on Cloudgate's orange trees, on the brass plate on the third step of the Fish Stairs. By morning it has gone, and Cloudgate is still in the sky, held up by a keepsake that is not its own, by leave of a wind who was given a letter two hundred years late, by a hand that had no business with it. Nobody at Halyard House knows who opened the escritoire. Lady Halyard has had the lock changed, and sat a long time in the drawing room with an empty box and a green ribbon in her lap, and has not sent for the Watch. The sylphs know, and are telling everyone. Nobody believes them; it is too good.".
  Ending end_undertow "The Undertow" when closing() and openedBelow(sennet_bay) "The Door of Doors was opened from below, by the whole of the Whelm with its hands on an Aenean wheel, and by you; and the Rose was never made. The four petals of it are where they are: in their courts, in your packs, in the keel of a hill in the sky. The Grand Tour is declared, by a House that does not know what else to declare, won. The lamps of the Whelm came back on at the turn of the next tide, row by row along the Undercroft, as if somebody were walking along them with a taper; and the merfolk came out of their houses into the green and looked at one another, and No Admittance said, in bubbles, that it had been a long time since anybody in the Whelm had done anything, and that she had liked it.".
  Ending end_found_out "Found Out" when closing() and rectorTaken(quance) "The Rector of the House of Doors is in the Watch-house on Fish Street, in a cell with a window on the bay, dictating to nobody. Sergeant Bassett brings her tea. She is perfectly charming to him. The Door of Doors stands in its round room at the top of the tower, shut, with the cat-flap of the tower door swinging, and the House has no Rector, and the Grand Tour is suspended, pending. Nobody has opened anything. Nobody has fallen out of the sky. The courts keep their petals, or you do, or a hill does. In Sennet they say it is the strangest Tour there has ever been, because nobody won it, and everybody seems to have come home.".
  Ending end_kept_above "Kept Above" when closing() and bribed(party) "Cloudgate is in the sky, at the top of its chain, in the sun, and it is always afternoon there, and it is held up by a thing it was given for love and used as a lever, and you know it, and have a hundred coin of the Halyards' money in your pack for knowing it. You are received at Halyard House whenever you go up. You find that you do not go up.".
  Ending end_unopened "The Tour Unfinished" when closing() "The eighth morning. The Door of Doors is shut at the top of the tower, and the Rose is in pieces where it was, and Cloudgate is in the sky, and the Whelm is under the bay, and the gulls stand on the bollards of the Quay as if they had been appointed to them. The Grand Tour goes on, the crier says, until somebody wins it. Somebody may. Sennet does not wait to see. It has eels to sell.".
}
